.SH NAME
md__2home_2umar_2Desktop_2WorkShop_2GLL_2bav_2README \- Baseconv 
.PP
 Converts to different numerical bases such as binary (2), octal (8) and hexadecimal (16) with built-in interactive shell\&.
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
base 2 -> for binary representation 0 to 1 
.br

.IP "\(bu" 2
base 8 -> for octal representation 0 to 7 base
.IP "\(bu" 2
base 16 -> for hexadecimal representation 0 to 15 (f)
.PP
.SS "Setting Up Ecosystem or Testing Environment"
There are two alternatives: 
.br
 1 - Build On your direct host 2 - Or on a dedicated environment (for testing and a quick tour)\&. 
.br
.PP
\fBThe ecosystem chosen to build the project is based on the \fCmeson build system\fP\fP 
.SH "1 - Direct Host"
.PP
It is therefore strongly recommended to have meson build available on your machine\&. Here are a few commands to type 
.PP
.nf
$ > meson setup build # to build the project 
$ > meson compile \-C build # to compile the project  

.fi
.PP
 The Executable w'ill be stored in build directory \fC\&./build/bcv\fP 
.br
.SH "2 - Pre-build or Dedicated Environment"
.PP
By the way, there's already a \fBpre-build environment\fP for those who want to test in Docker or Chroot\&. As you may have noticed, there's no Docker file in the repository, because we want to launch the environment quickly without doing a full Docker command\&.
.PP
We've already provided a script to export the environment to a local Docker image\&.~(no Docker build , no Docker pull no Docker anything)~ Here's a script named \fCbuild_context\&.sh\fP in the \fCcontext\fP directory\&. Just run the script\&. \fCbash @section autotoc_md9 go to the context directory and I assume you've given the right permission $ > \&./build_context\&.sh @icode This script will generate 2 environments of your choice: 1 - docker image directly available on your host, no need to docker pull just run\&.\&.\&. \fPbash $ docker run -ti bcv bash # to run  2 - A Change Root or chroot environment is available in most Unix environments\&. After \fCbuild_context\&.sh\fP has finished running, a \fCrootfs\fP directory will be available\&. To lock in : 
.PP
.nf
$ > sudo  chroot rootfs bash 

.fi
.PP
 And there you are! The command is available in whole environment just type \fCbcv -h\fP
.PP
See \fBUSE\fP section bellow for more info \&.
.SS "Use"
There are 2 modes of use An interactive mode & Simple command-line mode
.PP
*Simple Command-line mode
.PP
just do 
.br
 
.PP
.nf
bcv [option] <numeric value> 

.fi
.PP
 e\&.g 
.PP
.nf
$ bcv \-o 12 # for octal representation  

.fi
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
Type \fCbcv -h\fP to display help\&. 
.RE
.PP
.PP
*For interactive mode 
.br
.PP
If no argument is supplied, interactive mode is launched by default\&. here's an example:
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ bcv \-b/<number>  
$ bcv \-o/<number> 
$ bcv \-x/<number>   
.fi
.PP
 e\&.g:
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ bcv # launch in interactive mode 

[1] bcv > x/100
     |\-> 0x64
[2] bcv > b/100
     |\->0b0110\&.0100
[3] bcv > \&.\&.\&.
.fi
.PP
.PP
*To exit, type quit or exit (not case-sensitive)\&.
